  "tiles": [
    {
      "tile_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying",
      "title": "Matching and Simplifying Charts for an Audience",
      "summary": "Learn to choose the right chart type for the specific point you are making to a stakeholder audience, then simplify that chart by removing visual clutter so the operations manager can focus on the finding, not on reading the graph.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s01",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Chart Type to Point Matching",
          "title": "The chart must match the point, not just the data",
          "lede": "You have the delivery data. The question is: which chart makes your specific point land for the operations manager?",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s01.b1",
              "role": "intro",
              "text": "During your own exploration you might have built a bar chart, a line plot, and a boxplot of the delivery data. Presenting to the operations manager, the question changes: which one actually makes the Lagos point, for this audience? The same dataset can support several chart types \u2014 the right choice depends on the specific point you are communicating, not on the data alone.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb1"
                ],
                "fact_ids": []
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s01.b2",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "A bar chart is used for graphing categorical data and comparing values across categories \u2014 showing average delivery time side by side across Lagos, Abuja, and Kano. A line graph is best when you want to show how the value of something changes over time \u2014 showing Lagos delivery time rising week over week. Boxplots offer a condensed display for comparing distributions side by side, but a boxplot may be unfamiliar to many people and could introduce a learning curve; for a manager who hasn't seen one before, a bar chart or line plot can often make the point more directly.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f1",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f2",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f3",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f5"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "side_by_side",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s01.b3",
              "left_label": "Bar chart \u2014 compare values across cities",
              "left": {
                "type": "chart",
                "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s01.b3.left",
                "chart_kind": "bar",
                "asset_ref": {
                  "asset_id": "asset_city_deliveries",
                  "variant_id": null,
                  "columns": [
                    "city",
                    "avg_delivery_minutes"
                  ]
                },
                "title": "Average Delivery Time by City",
                "alt_text": "Bar chart showing average delivery time in minutes for Lagos (34), Abuja (22), and Kano (19), illustrating a city-vs-city comparison.",
                "encoding": {
