        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Chart Type to Point Matching",
          "title": "Pie charts, scatterplots, and the comparison trap",
          "lede": "Two more chart types complete the toolkit \u2014 and one common misconception about pie charts is worth clearing up.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03.b1",
              "role": "practical_context",
              "text": "A pie chart expresses a part-to-whole relationship in the data. It is best used either for a general sense of how parts relate to the whole, or to highlight that one segment is notably larger or smaller than the others. The pie must depict a meaningful whole \u2014 values that sum to 100%. If the point is 'Lagos accounts for most of our total delivery volume,' a pie chart of each city's share of total orders fits that part-to-whole framing in a way a bar chart of averages would not.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03.b2",
              "role": "practical_context",
              "text": "A scatterplot is often used to understand data rather than to communicate with it, though it can still be designed to convey findings to an audience. Keep this in mind if the distance/delivery-time relationship ever needs to go in front of the manager directly, rather than staying an exploratory chart.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f6"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s03.b3",
              "role": "misconception",
              "text": "It can look like comparing pie slices is just as easy as comparing bar lengths, since both show a value at a glance \u2014 but it is not. A bar chart's aligned, common baseline makes length differences easy to judge; two pie slices of similar size are harder to rank correctly by eye.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb5"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#m1"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "reward": {
            "gems": 5
          },
          "lineage": {
