        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s06",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Simplifying Charts for an Audience",
          "title": "Remove the clutter \u2014 not the data",
          "lede": "A chart built for your own exploration carries elements the operations manager doesn't need. Each removal reduces reading effort without changing the numbers.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s06.b1",
              "role": "intro",
              "text": "A bar chart of city delivery times built during your own exploration can carry gridlines, a border, and precise decimal labels that help you double-check the numbers. The operations manager probably doesn't need any of that to get the point. Gridlines can be deleted from a chart; if precise values matter to the audience, label the data point directly instead of relying on gridlines. Reducing clutter lets the audience use their attention to decide what actions might be warranted, rather than working out how to read the graph.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#tb1",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f1",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.s06.b2",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "A chart's border can be removed, since it is not adding informative value; white space can differentiate parts of a visual better than a border. Data labels should be used sparingly, only where the exact values are important to the audience; otherwise remove them and use the axis instead. Unnecessary trailing zeroes can be dropped from axis labels \u2014 '22.00 minutes' can become '22 min'. Diagonally rotated axis text is slower to read; city-name tick labels like Lagos, Abuja, and Kano don't need to be rotated at all.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#tb2",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f2",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f3",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f5",
                  "stakeholder.simplifying_charts_for_audience#f6"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
