      "remediation_steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r01",
          "type": "remediation",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
          "title": "Scatterplots: exploratory by default, communicative by design",
          "lede": "A scatterplot is a powerful tool for your own analysis \u2014 but it needs extra design work to communicate findings to an audience.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r01.b1",
              "role": "practical_context",
              "text": "A scatterplot is often used to understand data rather than to communicate with it. When you plot distance_km against delivery_minutes for orders across Lagos, Abuja, and Kano, you are exploring a relationship \u2014 that is the scatterplot's strength. It can still be designed to convey findings to an audience, but that requires deliberate choices: a clear title stating the finding, colour coding by city, and a trend line if the relationship is the point. Without those additions, a scatterplot of 15 order records is harder for the operations manager to read than a bar chart or line graph.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f6"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb4"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f6"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r02",
          "type": "remediation",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's revisit",
          "title": "Matching chart type to the point: a quick contrast",
          "lede": "The same delivery data can be shown as a bar chart, line graph, pie chart, or scatterplot \u2014 the right choice depends entirely on the point you are making.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r02.b1",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "Bar chart: comparing values across categories \u2014 which city has the longest average delivery time? Use it when the point is a side-by-side comparison (Lagos 34 min, Abuja 22 min, Kano 19 min). Line graph: showing change over time \u2014 is Lagos delivery time rising week over week? Pie chart: part-to-whole \u2014 does Lagos account for most of total orders? The pie must sum to 100%. Boxplot: comparing distributions side by side \u2014 but may be unfamiliar to a non-technical audience, so a bar chart or line plot often makes the point more directly. Scatterplot: exploring a relationship \u2014 distance vs. delivery time \u2014 but needs extra design work to communicate findings clearly.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb2",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb3",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f1",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f2",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f3",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f4",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f5",
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#f6"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stakeholder_chart_matching_simplifying.r02.b2",
              "role": "misconception",
              "text": "It can look like comparing pie slices is just as easy as comparing bar lengths \u2014 but it is not. A bar chart's aligned, common baseline makes length differences easy to judge; two pie slices of similar size are harder to rank correctly by eye. Use a pie chart only when the part-to-whole relationship is the point, not when precise comparison between categories is needed.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stakeholder.chart_type_to_point_matching#tb5"
                ],
